eld/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e21092-76b6-5a4c-b117-1001115db845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285321} {:answer/id #uuid "553afa5a-4338-5c57-b279-ff0312e09a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285569} {:answer/id #uuid "811b0f7f-9402-5653-bc54-13686f987440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{C}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285974} {:answer/id #uuid "e236c333-2153-5146-b193-5ef3d6ec8ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286848} {:answer/id #uuid "efd75d20-6069-5efc-a801-26f5c278e695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285321} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab7d9b8-1d47-56d4-a66f-0f80a739dcdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dab3d6dc-4b31-53f2-8ab3-bc64485c9198" :question/math-academy-id "q-181688" :question/problem "![](images/46/4628a6e45445a585361f7d32586b27e29bc726d4ed8b7f072679e5537df59644.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x+1\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,3x\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=x+1$ and *below* the line $y=3x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>x+1,\\,y<3x.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\nx+1<y<3x.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx+1<y\\le 3x\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+1<y\\le 3x\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2fbe4f7-be9b-5387-8aeb-28484f4af8cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9099832-9536-5764-b8f9-82d9f50c96e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409896} :answer-field/id #uuid "1979d639-7c7b-51e8-9e83-40721047de2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53607ff6-5913-50ea-923c-e269930ccd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409894} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cdacac-1881-5ec8-b9e9-d206d7013904" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d74e258e-f0dd-579c-9148-3c83bfa32b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409895} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1c0edf-ff99-50f4-9ecf-942f2ee605d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6164b43d-cb18-5fe7-a896-e7c6ff8846af" :question/math-academy-id "q-181810" :question/problem "![](images/59/592dc9d0d4713406cf9bf9c6ad966cb4446420113aa0e94fcfdcdb35b6471a9c.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<144\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5d4c010-f76c-5137-833c-1a7617a42cc7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420382}}] :activity/title "Describing Planar Regions Using Set-Builder Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420380}]}
